      "text": "To find the remainder when the sum of the four integers is divided by $11$, we start by defining the four integers in terms of their remainders when divided by $11$. Let the four integers be $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$. According to the problem, we have:\n\\[ a = 11k + 2 \\]\n\\[ b = 11m + 4 \\]\n\\[ c = 11n + 6 \\]\n\\[ d = 11p + 8 \\]\nfor some integers $k$, $m$, $n$, and $p$.\n\nThe sum of these four integers is:\n\\[ a + b + c + d = (11k + 2) + (11m + 4) + (11n + 6) + (11p + 8) \\]\n\\[ = 11(k + m + n + p) + 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 \\]\n\\[ = 11(k + m + n + p) + 20 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when $11(k + m + n + p) + 20$ is divided by $11$. Since $11(k + m + n + p)$ is a multiple of $11$, it leaves a remainder of $0$ when divided by $11$. Therefore, we only need to consider the remainder when $20$ is divided by $11$:\n\\[ 20 \\div 11 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 9 \\]\nSo, the remainder when $20$ is divided by $11$ is $9$. Thus, the remainder when the sum of the four integers is divided by $11$ is:\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]",
